# Ownership of the perimeter repository.
#
# The split matters: changing WHO is inside the perimeter is a routine, additive act; changing WHAT the
# perimeter protects, or what a shared rule template allows, affects every division at once.

# Default: nothing lands without the perimeter owners.
*                           @your-org/network-team @your-org/security-team

# Baseline — the services under protection, the dry-run window, the attribute budget.
# Both teams, always: this file is the definition of the boundary.
/perimeter/policy.yaml      @your-org/network-team @your-org/security-team

# Rule templates. A change here propagates to every division already using the profile, so it is reviewed
# like a shared library, not like a config tweak.
/perimeter/profiles/        @your-org/network-team @your-org/security-team

# Access levels — the context conditions (corporate network, managed device). Widening an IP range here
# quietly widens every rule that references it.
/perimeter/access-levels/   @your-org/network-team @your-org/security-team

# Which repository may request which projects. A team cannot widen its own list, because the list is not
# in its repository — that property only holds as long as this file needs the same approval as a profile.
/perimeter/contributors.yaml @your-org/network-team @your-org/security-team

# The contribution action used by other repositories. Changing it changes what those repositories send.
/contrib/                   @your-org/network-team @your-org/security-team

# Membership. The bot opens these PRs; the networking team approves.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE ONE PATH BY WHICH DATA MAY LEAVE GOOGLE CLOUD, AND SECURITY IS NOT AN OWNER OF IT.
# That is deliberate, and it is safe only because the control was moved OFF this file. Until 2026-08-12 it
# was not: the line below said Security is pulled in when an entry carries an `exceptions:` block, and no
# workflow ever read that field — the field itself rendered nothing (DEC-23). A promise in a comment is
# worth exactly what a promise in a comment is worth.
#
# WHAT GATES IT NOW. A member entry that selects a `risk: high` profile (one whose egress rule leaves
# Google Cloud) and supplies a destination is rejected by `policy/onboarding.rego` unless
# `perimeter/policy.yaml` §egress_approvals carries a live approval naming that member, that profile and
# those exact destinations. `policy.yaml` IS owned by Security, two lines above. So the approval lands in
# a file Security owns, instead of in a file it does not.
#
# WHY NOT CODEOWNERS. Three reasons, each sufficient: (1) the ordinary request and the high-risk request
# are the same line in the same file, so path matching cannot tell them apart — splitting by CONTENT is a
# policy rule, not an ownership rule; (2) at ~50 requests a month, putting Security on every one of them
# produces a review that gets rubber-stamped and then removed; (3) without branch protection
# (`403 Upgrade to GitHub Pro` on a private repository in the free plan) CODEOWNERS is not enforced by
# GitHub at all, while the OPA rule runs on both tracks — pull request AND apply (DEC-16).
#
# SO WHAT IS THIS FILE FOR. It states who is expected to read what, and it is the enforcement mechanism on
# GitHub Enterprise, where branch protection exists. It is NOT the mechanism any security property of this
# repository depends on. `tools/codeowners_check.py` keeps that honest: it fails when the file that carries
# Security's approvals is not owned by anyone beyond the owners of the file being approved.
#
# ONE FILE MEANS ONE OWNER, AND THAT IS A DECISION, NOT A SIDE EFFECT (DEC-12). Membership used to be one
# file per project, so `/perimeter/members/risk-* @your-org/risk-team` was AVAILABLE — nobody had configured
# it, but the option existed. With a single file, path-based ownership per division is gone: CODEOWNERS
# matches paths, and every division's entry lives at the same path.
#
# WHAT IS ACTUALLY LOST — one future option, not a control that was in place. What a division may ask for
# has never been decided by file ownership; it is decided by the CHANNEL:
#   * `snow:` — the ticket is verified by calling ServiceNow back, and the answer names the project,
#   * `pr:`  — `perimeter/contributors.yaml` maps repository to allowed projects, and that file lives HERE,
#   * `manual:` — an approval under this very file, which is the only channel CODEOWNERS ever gated.
# So "a division cannot onboard somebody else's project" holds exactly as before, and it never came from
# this line.
#
# WHAT THIS COSTS AT THIRTY DIVISIONS: every membership change needs a review from the team below, which is
# already true today, and self-service stays "the division opens a request, the platform approves" rather
# than becoming "the division approves its own entry". If that ever has to change, the exit is a directory
# shard (`perimeter/projects/<division>.yaml`) plus a renderer that reads `**/*.yaml` — a change of ADDRESSES
# in Terraform state, so it goes as its own pull request with `moved{}`, never in passing.
/perimeter/projects.yaml    @your-org/network-team

# The renderer and the gates. Somebody who can edit these can neutralise every other line in this file.
/terraform/                 @your-org/network-team @your-org/security-team
/policy/                    @your-org/security-team
/tools/                     @your-org/network-team @your-org/security-team
/.github/workflows/         @your-org/network-team @your-org/security-team
